CHAPTER 14
ADR and the Future of Procedure
So far in this book, we have focused on the process of formal litigation within the confines of the public courthouse. It is this process for the resolution of civil actions that constitutes what we usually call “civil procedure.” But far from all, or even most, civil disputes become civil actions—with complaints filed, summonses served, and all the other process that litigation entails. Most dispute resolution occurs outside the courtroom and without the need for any court’s rules of civil procedure.

Disputes can come to an out-of-court end in simple ways: A person with a grievance may “lump it” rather than go through the hassle of demanding redress; an aggrieved person may approach the apparent wrongdoer, and after talking things over, the two individuals may sort things out in a mutually satisfactory way. Or an out-of-court resolution could dwarf most court proceedings in complexity, expense, and duration: two multinational corporations could agree to resolve a multi-billion-dollar dispute before a panel of three private arbitrators from around the world, each charging thousands of dollars a day, and conduct dozens of hearings (involving dozens of lawyers) over a period of years before the panel of arbitrators deliberates and renders a decision. From the simplest to the most complex, these out-of-court means for resolving legal disputes exist as alternatives to litigation, and collectively they are known as “alternative dispute resolution” or “ADR.”

To fully understand civil procedure—both to understand how it works and to assess whether it works well—requires understanding the alternatives to litigation that disputants have available to them. In the preceding chapters of Part IV of this book, we have seen how a person with a legal claim may face a choice to file in state court or federal court, or to file in the courts of one state or another state, or to seek to litigate using the rules of one court system
1308
or another. Even more fundamentally, a person with a legal claim has a choice whether to file in court at all. Litigation is just one option among many for resolving a dispute. Choosing the right alternative for the needs of your client is an important part of lawyering.

We begin this chapter, therefore, with an introduction to ADR. Of the many forms of ADR, we will study one in depth: arbitration. Arbitration, like litigation, involves parties submitting their legal claims to a neutral decisionmaker who reviews arguments and evidence and renders a decision announcing who wins, and how much they win. In these ways, arbitration is a close analogue to litigation, and its basic structure will be familiar to you. Yet arbitration, precisely because it is not litigation, is not subject to the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, and features of arbitral procedure can vary dramatically from litigation. The proceedings may be secret, rather than in public; the parties may choose a group of arbitrators, rather than being assigned a single judge; there may be no discovery; there may be no appeal.

The ways that arbitral procedure can diverge from court procedure remind us that there is nothing fixed or inevitable about the rules and doctrines of civil procedure that we have studied in this book. There are other ways to structure dispute resolution than the ways that the courts we’ve studied have chosen. And civil procedure tomorrow need not look like civil procedure today. The rules and doctrines that we have studied in this book are the product of a long history that reflects both continuity and change. Rules will continue to evolve. Indeed, in your legal career, you may become a force behind future changes in the law.

Procedure can change—but to what end? To answer this question, we return one last time to the two main themes of this book. First: Lawyering matters, because procedure is power. How can lawyers use procedure to serve their clients’ goals? To serve their own goals? To serve society’s goals? How could procedure better align these goals? Second: Parties are entitled to due process—but how much process is due? This chapter presents one last Rule to read: Rule 1. It directs—or at least aspires—that procedure be “just, speedy, and inexpensive.” Can procedure be all three? Or are these goals inevitably in conflict? Thus, this chapter and this book end with a discussion of the unresolved tensions inherent in the design of the procedural systems we have studied, attention to enduring and unsolved challenges for the effective and
1309
fair operation of civil justice, and an invitation to consider possibilities for the future of civil procedure.

