Alternative Dispute Resolution
Throughout this book, we have already encountered the most common and most important form of ADR: settlement. If the parties to a dispute can agree on how to resolve it, there is no need for litigation. Even when parties go to court, the benefits of settlement—ending the conflict, avoiding further expense, and dispelling the uncertainty of trial—lead most cases to end with settlement, not trial.

The distinguishing feature of settlement as a form of ADR is that it is a process of negotiation instead of adjudication. No judge, jury, or decisionmaker of any kind decides who wins. Rather, the parties come to a mutual agreement or else there is no settlement.

There are other forms of ADR, which to varying degrees involve more process and formality than individual negotiation and settlement. These run the gamut from mediation, which is settlement negotiation facilitated by a third party, to various procedures that private companies use to resolve disputes among their customers or clients (dubbed “platform procedure”), to arbitration, a process by which parties contractually agree to be bound by the decision of a third party (called the arbitrator) who will hear their claims and then render a binding decision (called an award).

Notably, ADR is not necessarily an “alternative” to litigation in the sense that ADR and litigation are mutually exclusive options. Rather, ADR can be part of the litigation process. A case may begin in traditional court, proceed through discovery, then go to mediation, whether by consent of the parties or by order of the court. If the mediation fails, then the parties continue to trial. Or a case may be filed in court, but then the judge may order arbitration based on a preexisting agreement between the parties to arbitrate any disputes. And settlement, of course, is always an option for parties in litigation. Thus, practices like mediation and arbitration are alternatives to litigation, but they need not be entirely separate from litigation.

Methods of ADR developed as potential solutions to the shortcomings of formal litigation. Advocates of ADR have emphasized the formality, expense, and emotional toll associated with litigation. These problems are interrelated and mutually reinforcing. Civil procedure is highly formalized: notice must be given in precise ways, arguments must be raised at certain times or else be waived, briefs must be a certain length, and so on. The dry technicality of court
1311
procedure may be jarring to litigants, who feel that the proceedings are disconnected from their flesh-and-blood experiences and injuries. Formality also distances them from the process, as compliance with these formalities requires legal skill—which usually means hiring a lawyer—and costs time and money. Cases may take years to resolve in court, and the stress—financial, mental, and emotional—of ongoing litigation interferes with litigants’ lives.

Proponents of ADR have therefore advocated for processes like negotiation, mediation, and arbitration as less formal, faster, less expensive, and less stressful ways to resolve disputes. Even when the emotional toll of litigation is less of a concern, such as when a dispute arises between businesses, ADR can save costs, avoid unnecessary confrontation, preserve business relationships, protect trade secrets, and allow for solutions that are better tailored to the needs of the parties than a generalist judge may be able to craft.

Below, we briefly discuss three of the most significant forms of ADR (settlement, mediation, and platform procedure) before examining in greater detail arbitration, which today is the most prominent and controversial form of ADR. As you read about these forms of ADR, ask yourself: How have they responded to the shortcomings of formal litigation? Do they achieve the aims of ADR noted above? Or do they serve different ends entirely?

NOTE ON NEGOTIATION AND SETTLEMENT
Settlements can be among the most complex transactions in the legal world, as when government regulators and a multinational corporation agree, after a decades-long antitrust investigation, that the corporation will undertake a series of asset sales, divestitures, and business restructurings to address the regulators’ concerns about the corporation’s ability to limit competition in the industry. They can be simple and commonplace, as when two pedestrians collide, knocking one to the ground (which, technically, is a battery), and after being asked, “I’m so sorry, is there anything I can do to make it up to you?,” the pedestrian who took a tumble says, “No worries,” and continues on his way.

By many criteria, settlement is the best form of ADR. It is usually faster and less expensive for the parties. It requires no time or resources from anyone beyond the parties themselves. As a settlement is the product of mutual
1312
agreement, it gives both parties control over the outcome. It avoids the uncertainty of having a third party decide the matter in way that leaves one or both parties disappointed. It avoids the stressful and potentially embarrassing spectacle of public hearings on the dispute, and it gives the parties an opportunity to maintain whatever prior relationship they had without further confrontation. And if the merit of the grievance is clear, both parties may recognize that there is no point to further conflict.

Unsurprisingly, then, settlement is the outcome for the vast majority of legal disputes that arise in the world. Most disputes end without a lawyer ever hearing about them; even when lawyers get involved, they usually settle before an action is filed in court; and even when a matter goes to court, the litigation tends to end in settlement.

Settlement has its drawbacks, though. Privacy may be bug, not a feature. Through the presentation of evidence and the announcement of legal judgments, the litigation process can educate society about wrongdoing and its consequences. And even in cases with no ramifications beyond the parties, a settlement may not reflect the true merits of the grievance being settled. For example, if one party is very risk averse, such that they strongly wish to avoid the uncertainty that comes with litigation, or they lack the resources to litigate the case effectively, they may agree to a settlement that, relative to the merits of their claim, is highly unfavorable.

For example, let’s say that D has injured P in the amount of $10,000, but it is unclear whether D will be legally liable for the injury. The parties both believe that there is a 50% chance that D will be held liable in court. A fair settlement might be $5,000. This would reflect the expected value of the claim (in other words, the amount of the claim, discounted by the probability that the plaintiff will lose) and would save both parties the time and expense of litigation. But if P is very risk averse and D is not, P might accept a settlement of only $1,000. This might be acceptable to P and D, but it is problematic from the point of view of the legal system. If injurers only pay a fraction of their expected liability in settlements, this weakens the deterrent effect of the law.

Conversely, to modify the example above, what if P claims that D injured him in the amount of $3,000, but this is dubious as a legal matter. P has, at best, only a 10% chance of winning in court. A fair settlement might be, at most, $300. But let’s say that D is highly risk averse, or D does not have
1313
enough money to hire a lawyer to defend the claim in court. D might settle for $1,000—an amount he can barely afford.

And then there is most common form of settlement: settlement in the barest sense of the term, without negotiation or relief—colloquially, “lumping it.” You suffer an injury. You believe that you have a legal right to relief, but the cost of pursuing it is too high, or the benefit of pursuing it is too low, or the chance of ultimately obtaining a meaningful settlement or judgment too remote. So you do not pursue the matter, not even to the point of complaining to anyone.

Sometimes, lumping it is the right outcome for everyone. Like the example of the pedestrians bumping on the sidewalk, some legal wrongs aren’t worth pursuing—even if the court system were free, fast, and perfectly accurate in its decisionmaking. Other times, lumping it is a bitter blow—both to the victim of the injury and to the civil justice system. If a harm is significant, but the victim is discouraged by the cost and difficulty of obtaining relief, it means that rights that exist “on the books” don’t have effect “on the ground”—in other words, where it matters most.

In dry legalese, a legal claim that costs too much to be worthwhile to bring is a negative expected value claim. A numerical example will illustrate the term. Let’s say that D has injured P in the amount of $1,000, and there is an 80% chance that D will be held liable in court. This is a strong legal claim, but will P pursue the claim? If the cost in terms of money, time, and stress is worth more than $800, then no. To the injured party, the expected value—$800 expected recovery minus the costs of getting the expected recovery—is less than zero.

Civil procedure affects whether legal claims have negative expected value or positive expected value. Procedural rules, and the litigation strategies that parties can employ, affect the cost, delay, uncertainty, and stress of litigation. They affect the likelihood that a theoretical right to relief will become an actual judgment awarding relief. They affect when, where, with whom, against whom, in what court, and under what law litigation will occur. As you review the doctrines and concepts that you have learned from the book, consider the various ways that the procedures we have studied in this book help turn negative expected value claims into positive expected value claims—or vice versa.

Finally, and as noted above, there are many reasons why parties settle. But of course some disputes do not settle. Why not? Think about what might lead
1314
parties not to reach a settlement. As you read the note below on mediation, consider how mediation might help parties overcome obstacles to settlement.

NOTE ON MEDIATION
Mediation is a special form of settlement negotiation in which a neutral third party with no interest in the case—the mediator—acts as an intermediary between the parties. The mediator attempts to help the parties find common ground and reach a mutually agreeable settlement. She may also facilitate settlement by providing a neutral assessment of each party’s case, which may prompt a stubborn party to come to the bargaining table. Mediators come from many backgrounds, not all of them legal. But it is integral to the mediation process that the mediator be respected and seen as neutral and competent. Accordingly, retired judges and attorneys who formerly practiced in the relevant field are often selected as mediators. Mediation began as a way to resolve disputes between family members, neighbors, and acquaintances without the acrimony of a full-blown lawsuit, but it has since become popular in purely commercial disputes.

Mediation can occur by the consent of the parties or by court order. Importantly, the mediator does nothing that binds the parties. Thus, mediation—even when it is made “mandatory” by court order—is simply settlement negotiation that is guided by a third party. That said, the presence of a trusted third party is sometimes enough to make settlement possible when it otherwise would fail.

Why would the parties agree to bring in another person—who has her own fees that must be paid—to achieve settlement, which they can theoretically accomplish on their own? One reason is trust: if the parties deeply distrust each other, they may be unwilling to share accurate information or make concessions toward middle ground, out of fear that the other side will not reciprocate. If a mediator has the trust of the parties, however, they can share information about the case, or about their willingness to settle, with the mediator, and the mediator can promise to share the information with the other side only if the other side is also willing to be candid with the mediator. Once parties start sharing openly with the mediator and see that the other side is doing the same, they can build enough trust in each other—or at least in the process—to work toward settlement.

1315
Another, and related, reason for mediation is divergent beliefs. Most people suffer from some degree of “optimism bias,” meaning that they overestimate their likelihood of success at tasks. If both parties overestimate the strength of their case, this can prevent settlement. A simple numerical example can illustrate this idea. Imagine that P has a claim for $120,000 against D. P can sue, and if she does, she optimistically expects to have an 80% chance of winning, and her lawyer tells her that litigating the case will cost $20,000. She doesn’t mind the risk of losing at trial—she is not risk averse—but she does mind paying attorney fees if she can avoid them. So, to save the cost of litigation, P first tries to settle with D. How much will she demand in settlement? That depends on her negotiation strategy, but note that she would not ask for less than $76,000. This is because at trial, P expects to win, on average, $96,000 (in other words, her $120,000 claim times an 80% chance of winning) but pay $20,000 in attorney fees, for a net recovery of $76,000.

Now look at the settlement negotiation from the perspective of D. D optimistically expects that P only has a 40% chance of winning at trial, and D’s lawyer tells him that defending the case will cost $20,000. Like P, D does not mind the risk of trial but wants to avoid paying attorney fees. How much would D be willing to pay in settlement? Not a dime more than $68,000. This is because at trial, D expects to lose, on average, $48,000 (in other words, the $120,000 claim times a 40% chance of P winning) plus $20,000 in attorney fees. Settling for more than $68,000 is worse for D than risking trial.

In this example, the parties will fail to settle. P won’t accept less than $76,000. D won’t pay more than $68,000. Importantly, this stalemate is not due to bluffing or hard bargaining by either party (although of course those strategies could prevent settlement, too!). Each party genuinely believes that their final offer is the absolute farthest they can go before trial looks better than settlement for them, even after accounting for the cost of litigation. In litigation, this stalemate can sometimes be broken by discovery, which can help the parties gain a common understanding of the case. Advice from the judge can help, too. But litigation is expensive, and mediation may be a faster and less expensive alternative.

Note that to achieve the goal of settlement, the mediator does not need the parties to reach a perfect understanding about the dispute—rather, the mediator only needs to find enough common ground to make settlement possible. To continue the example, let’s say that P and D hire a mediator, and thanks
1316
to the mediation process, both parties have tempered their optimism, but only partially: P expects to have a 70% chance of winning at trial, while D puts P’s chances at 50%. They still see the case differently, but settlement is possible. P is willing to accept a settlement of $64,000 now, while D is willing to pay as much as $80,000 to avoid trial.* A settlement amount of $70,000 or $75,000 would be agreeable to both parties, even though it would not have been before mediation.

NOTE ON PLATFORM PROCEDURE
Suppose you buy a phone charger from a private seller on eBay for $10. When it arrives, it doesn’t work. You go online and request a refund. Rather than issue the refund and accept the charger back, the seller claims it was working when she sent it, so you must be lying. This dispute will be resolved, at first, by eBay’s policies governing disputes between buyers and sellers. Similarly, disputes between users of websites like Facebook, Instagram, Airbnb, YouTube, and Amazon will be resolved in the first instance by the platform itself. Accordingly, Professor Rory Van Loo has termed this new and increasingly common mode of dispute resolution “platform procedure.” See Rory Van Loo, Federal Rules of Platform Procedure, 88 U. CHI. L. REV. (forthcoming 2021).

Platform procedure can play an important role in resolving disputes well before litigation ever enters the picture. In the phone charger hypothetical, for example, suppose eBay sided with you and forced the seller to refund you. Could the seller file a claim in small claims court for $10? Absolutely. Will she? Almost certainly not. Moreover, because platforms create their own codes of conduct, platform procedure can address disputes that courts cannot. Professor Van Loo gives the example that Facebook suspended hundreds of accounts owned by women for posting variations on the phrase “men are scum,” which violated Facebook’s hate speech policy. A party upset by these statements would not have a case in traditional litigation, but they can petition Facebook to remove them as violative of the platform’s rules.

1317
As you can see, platform procedure is a far cry from mediation! Platform procedure is a standardized process for all users of a platform; mediation is individualized and used by parties who seek it. Platform procedure decides winners and losers; mediation merely facilitates settlement. Platform procedure has rules set by the platform; in mediation, the parties have the final word on everything. But what platform procedure and mediation have in common is that they are both ADR—alternatives to court.

Platform procedure presents its own complications and drawbacks. First, violations of platform rules may generate formal disputes where there would otherwise be none—as in the “men are scum” example—which the platforms then must resolve. Second, concerns of fairness, participation, and autonomy may take a back seat to concern for profit when private companies decide disputes. We might expect Facebook, for example, to favor resolutions that keep its platform palatable to advertisers, since advertising is how Facebook generates revenue. Yet the profit motive could also discipline platforms to behave in ways widely perceived as fair. After all, Facebook also needs to avoid appearing overly concerned with advertisers at the expense of users so as to avoid public outcry and the loss of users. Similarly, we might expect marketplaces like eBay and Amazon to favor resolutions that drive sales. But would biased decisions help sales? On the one hand, if potential buyers know the platform will take their side in most disputes, they will be more likely to buy. But on the other hand, in this case, sellers will be less likely to sell their products on the platform. Thus, the presence of profit motives complicates our ability to predict how platforms will decide disputes. Third, platform procedure is private procedure—not merely in the sense that it is operated by private companies, but that the proceedings themselves are conducted privately, even confidentially. The rules of the resolution process need not be publicized, and companies may make exceptions or change rules whenever they see fit.

Nonetheless, platform procedure forms the first, and in many cases, only step of the dispute-resolution process for many disputes arising between participants in online platforms. As online marketplaces and networks continue to grow in importance, so will platform procedure.

