INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON ARBITRATION
Just as one has a right to a “day in court,” one also has the right to agree not to have a day in court. This option is presented most starkly in the context of arbitration. In an arbitration agreement, parties to a dispute contractually agree to resolve their dispute out of court, using a forum, decisionmaker, and procedures of their choosing.

Because arbitration is governed by contract, parties can choose procedures that are as different from or similar to court procedures as they wish. In many areas of arbitration practice, arbitration is often nearly indistinguishable from litigation: a typical arbitration will involve the claimant filing a complaint (called a demand in this context) and the parties engaging in discovery, filing motions and briefs, and attending hearings. The arbitrator may dismiss claims in the demand, hear oral arguments, issue a written opinion (the award), and order the payment of attorney fees and costs to a prevailing party. The only fundamental difference is that the decisionmaker is not a judge but an arbitrator, and the binding force of the arbitral award comes from the party’s arbitration agreement, not the judicial power of a court.

Arbitration is big business. In modern practice, arbitration has become a separate practice area unto itself. Domestic and international arbitration rival formal legal systems in scale and economic significance. Large organizations such as the American Arbitration Association provide arbitration services and offer rosters of potential arbitrators whom parties can select. Crucially, arbitration organizations are private entities and arbitrators are private individuals. In this way, arbitration is an “alternative” to litigation, even if the procedures followed by the parties in arbitration resemble court procedures.

Arbitration is not completely disconnected from litigation, though. Arbitration can lead to litigation if a party to an arbitration agreement refuses to arbitrate or refuses to comply with an arbitrator’s award. A federal statute, the Federal Arbitration Act, and analogous state statutes permit a party to go to court to enforce an arbitration agreement or arbitration award. They also permit a party who believes they should not be bound by an arbitral award to go to court to vacate the award. Because these statutes exist to facilitate the arbitration of disputes, they favor enforcement of arbitration agreements and awards, and the grounds for undoing an arbitral award are strictly limited.

1319
There are many kinds of arbitration. Virtually any dispute that can arise between people who are also parties to a contract can be the subject of arbitration. Arbitration, in one form or another, is a very popular form of dispute resolution in many areas, including disputes between private individuals, between large corporations and their customers, between labor unions and management, between employees and employers, between suppliers and purchasers in a product supply chain, between professional athletes and professional sports teams, and between multinational corporations and the governments of nations in which they make major business investments.

In all these contexts, it is common for parties to a contract to include in their contract an arbitration clause requiring that in the event a dispute arises, the parties must arbitrate the dispute rather than go to court. These are known as mandatory arbitration agreements. Mandatory arbitration agreements reflect not only a decision to pursue ADR, but a decision to forgo litigation. Because arbitration is a creature of contract, not all arbitration agreements are mandatory, nor are all arbitration agreements made before a dispute arises. Parties are always free after a dispute arises to mutually agree to arbitrate rather than litigate.

It is also worth distinguishing between commercial, or business-to-business, arbitration and consumer arbitration, meaning arbitration between businesses and their customers, which is usually governed by the “terms of sale” or “terms of use” governing the products and services sold by the business. Of these two types of arbitration, consumer arbitration has been the more controversial by far. Critics of consumer arbitration point out that the company usually writes the contract and presents it to the consumer on a take-it-or-leave-it basis (what is called a contract of adhesion). This raises the concern that arbitration may be desired only by the business, not the consumer, as an alternative to court. Further, arbitration, precisely because it is not litigation, does not generate legal precedent. Indeed, an arbitration agreement may require confidentiality, meaning that alleged wrongdoing by the company may not be publicized in the way that it would in litigation. In response to these criticisms, proponents of consumer arbitration point to the benefits of speed, lower cost, and informality that arbitration offers relative to litigation. They note that the costs of wasteful or frivolous litigation are passed on to consumers in the form of higher prices.

Commercial arbitration has been less controversial for a variety of reasons: commercial disputants are more likely to have roughly equal bargaining power when entering into an arbitration agreement, and arbitration has long
1320
been used for dispute resolution in commercial settings with a record of success. Consumer arbitration, in contrast, is comparatively new. Of course, the distinction between business-to-business arbitration and business-to-consumer arbitration is imperfect. Consider a contract between a large and powerful credit card company and a small, family-owned restaurant that wants to accept credit cards from its patrons. Both are commercial entities, but the terms of the contract between them, including any arbitration agreement, will be set entirely by the credit card company.

As you read the cases below, consider the following questions: What is gained when parties seek to resolve their disputes in arbitration rather than going to court? What is lost? And what is the effect of an award issued by an arbitrator? After all, the arbitrator is not sitting as a judge in a court of law. If a party is dissatisfied with an arbitral award, what recourse do they have?
